              "text": "Fossilized poop is scientifically known as a **coprolite** (derived from the Greek words *kopros*, meaning dung, and *lithos*, meaning stone). \n\nWhile it might sound like a joke, coprolites are incredibly valuable scientific treasures. They offer a unique, direct window into the diets, health, and ecosystems of creatures that lived millions of years ago. \n\n---\n\n### Why Do Scientists Study It?\nWhile skeletal fossils tell us what ancient animals *looked like*, coprolites tell us how they *lived and behaved*. By studying fossilized feces, paleoscatologists (scientists who study ancient poop) can discover:\n* **Diet:** Coprolites often contain preserved, undigested remains of what the animal ate, such as bone fragments, fish scales, feathers, seeds, and plant material.\n* **Parasites and Disease:** They can contain fossilized parasite eggs or ancient bacteria, revealing what kind of intestinal issues prehistoric animals faced.\n* **Ecosystems:** Knowing who was eating what allows scientists to reconstruct ancient food webs and understand the environment of the time.\n\n### How Does Poop Fossilize?\nBecause feces are organic and soft, they normally decay very quickly. For poop to fossilize, it must be buried rapidly in an environment with very little oxygen (like the muddy bottom of a lake or seabed) before it can be decomposed by bacteria or eaten by scavengers. \n\nOver thousands or millions of years, the organic matter is slowly replaced by minerals\u2014most commonly calcium phosphate or silicates. This process, called mineralization, turns the poop into a solid stone while perfectly preserving its original shape, texture, and contents.\n\n---\n\n### Famous Coprolites\n\n* **The Lloyds Bank Coprolite:** Discovered in York, England, in 1972 during the excavation of an old Viking settlement, this is the **largest fossilized human poop** ever found. Measuring 20 centimeters (8 inches) long and 5 centimeters (2 inches) wide, it belonged to a 9th-century Viking. Analysis showed he ate a diet of mostly meat and bread, and his gut was severely riddled with parasitic whipworms and maw-worms. One archaeologist famously stated that, in its own way, the specimen was \"as irreplaceable as the Crown Jewels.\"\n* **\"Barnum\" the T. Rex Poop:** This is the largest carnivore coprolite ever discovered. Weighing 20.5 pounds (9.28 kg) and measuring over two feet long, it is believed to have been left by a *Tyrannosaurus rex*. It contains heavily fractured fossilized bones, proving that T. rex crushed and ate the bones of its prey.\n\n---\n\n### The Poozeum\nIf you are fascinated by fossilized poop, there is an entire museum dedicated to it. Located in Williams, Arizona, the **Poozeum** is a free museum featuring thousands of coprolites. It was founded by George Frandsen, who holds the Guinness World Record for the largest collection of fossilized feces (over 8,000 specimens).",
